  "dom_id": "step-q86170",
  "name": "Question 3",
  "problem": "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14068433/assets/3ec66e7d27a46ad991e1aa10281a9850c500ba649b9699834fd867247b665376.png)\n\nThe graph of $y=f(x),$ shown above, consists of two line segments. Find the value of ${\\int }_{-4}^{5}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x.$",
  "worked_solution": "The graph consists of two parts: the line segment for $x\\in [-4,1)$ and the line segment for $x\\in (1,5].$ Therefore, we can write the required integral as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{-4}^{5}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x & ={{{\\int }_{-4}^{1}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x}_{\u23df}}_{-{A}_{1}}+{{{\\int }_{1}^{5}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x}_{\u23df}}_{{A}_{2}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- The $1$st integral represents the signed area between the curve and the $x$-axis, where $x\\in [-4,1).$ The corresponding region is a rectangle, so we find the area using the formula\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}_{1} & =ab \\\\  & =5(1) \\\\  & =5.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince the region lies below the $x$-axis, we have\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{-4}^{1}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x=-{A}_{1}=-5.\n$$\n\n- The $2$nd integral represents the signed area between the curve and the $x$-axis, where $x\\in (1,5].$ The corresponding region is a trapezoid, so we find the area using the formula\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}_{2} & =\\frac{1}{2}(a+b)h \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}(1+4)(4) \\\\  & =10.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{-4}^{5}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x & =-{A}_{1}+{A}_{2} \\\\  & =-5+10 \\\\  & =5.\\end{aligned}\n$$",
